The bug is the end index. `slice(start, end)` excludes `end`, so `arr.length - 1` drops the last element.

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(arr.length - n, arr.length);
}

console.log(lastN([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)); // [4, 5]
```

Or, more simply:

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(-n);
}
```

The `-n` version fails when `n` is 0, since `slice(-0)` is `slice(0)` and returns the whole array. If `n` can be 0, use the first version, or:

```js
function lastN(arr, n) {
  return arr.slice(Math.max(arr.length - n, 0));
}
```

This also handles `n` larger than the array length, which would otherwise produce a negative start index.

